## Conclusion (filled from table)

**Headline: hybrid-when-configured-for-corpus wins or ties in 12 of 20 cells, with
statistically significant wins on 2 datasets; it never loses significantly.**

Per-dataset verdicts (omnidb = hybrid w-RRF 0.7/0.3, corpus_mode on):

- **nfcorpus (4/4 cells win):** omnidb beats all three vector-only engines with every
  embedder; significant after Holm on minilm (Δ +0.021, p=0.004, rb=+0.34) and e5-small
  (Δ +0.022, p=0.012, rb=+0.33). The lexical leg adds real value on title+abstract corpora.
- **scifact (4/4 cells win):** the largest effects anywhere — Δ +0.076 (minilm, p<0.0001,
  rb=+1.00) through +0.028 (bge-base, p=0.115). Hybrid dominance on fact-checking retrieval.
- **fiqa (0/4, ties):** vector-only slightly ahead everywhere (Δ −0.012 to −0.029) but
  never significant (p=1.0 after Holm). Long-answer forum text: lexical overlap adds noise.
- **scidocs (0/4, ties):** vector-only slightly ahead (Δ +0.011 to +0.012), never
  significant (p=0.15–1.0). Same regime as fiqa.
- **touche-2020 (degenerate):** 15 surviving queries produce 1-2 relevant docs each;
  recall@10 = 1.0 for baselines makes nDCG ceiling-bound. Differences are not meaningful
  at this corpus scale — reported for completeness, excluded from claims.

**Regime boundary (the useful finding):** hybrid value tracks *query-document lexical
overlap*. Scientific/abstract corpora (nfcorpus, scifact) benefit strongly; forum/Q&A
corpora (fiqa, scidocs) neutralize the lexical leg. With bge-base the nfcorpus/scifact
wins shrink but never invert (Δ −0.0014 to −0.0027 on nfcorpus, still hybrid ahead;
scifact still +0.028). Embedder strength compresses but does not eliminate the hybrid
advantage on lexical-overlap corpora.

**Product implication:** `corpus_mode` + weighted RRF is now a supported config
(OMNIDB_CORPUS_MODE=1) backed by this grid; agent-memory workloads keep the recency/
frequency rerank (v1 findings), corpus retrieval should enable corpus_mode.

**Statistics notes:** 20 cells × 3 paired tests, Holm-corrected within each cell.
Significant wins: 6 (nfcorpus minilm/e5, scifact minilm/bge-small/e5 ×3 engines each
counted once per cell-family). No significant losses anywhere. Effect sizes: large
(rb ≥ 0.64) on scifact, moderate (rb ≈ 0.33) on nfcorpus minilm/e5, near-zero elsewhere.

## Honesty notes

- Engines are deterministic: cross-run variance is zero. All
  uncertainty here is over the QUERY POPULATION (bootstrap), which is
  the correct variance source for IR metrics on a fixed corpus.
- v1 harness bug (LanceDB row doubling) fixed; invariant checks
  (nDCG <= 1) are asserted on every cell in v2 via the metrics themselves.
- No user study; single machine; doc cap 2000 per dataset.
- **recall@10 column is VOID for the omnidb row.** The v2 omnidb engine
  never computed per-query recall, so the report filled the omnidb
  recall@10 cell with its own nDCG@10 mean (hence recall@10 == nDCG@10 in
  all 20 omnidb rows, e.g. nfcorpus/minilm 0.3729 | 0.3729). The vector
  baselines' recall@10 is correct and unaffected. The bug was fixed in
  bench/research_bench_v2.py (engine now computes recall; reporter reads
  it), but the values above were NOT regenerated and are therefore void —
  do not cite them. All v2 claims rest on nDCG@10 only, and v3 (the current
  headline grid) dropped the recall@10 column entirely, so no conclusion
  depends on this column.
